king for example the  more thorough deregulation of the  railroads there will be active  discussions of the extent to which we  can have more competition in the  maritime shipping field rather than move  toward tighter and tighter Karla's ation  the area of agricultural restrictions on  output now you know don't expect the  Millennium  but certainly one can move in the  direction always of the less restrictive  approach toward maintaining farm income  trade restrictions after all the  president just vetoed that cotton  textiles protective legislation he  vetoed the beef quota bill that would  have tied his hands more perhaps that  gives you enough I mean we're interested  in the state regulation of insurance I  mean do you know for example that in the  states in which you have opened filing  that is more of a possibility of price  competition that you have markedly lower  insurance rates for liability and  property insurance than you have in  other states there's a whole it's kind  of a general attitude toward every way  in which the government subsidizes and  protects and limits competition and I  expect to produce a result yes doctor I  don't see that this is really a change  from what government has tried to do  always in terms of trying to affect the  economy and in terms of results I don't  anticipate that there will be results in  the next twelve months from these types  of programs well I look I don't promise  miracles but we had an airline  regulation system for 40 years and we  changed it this year maybe people will  learn that it's not so horrible after  all we can make more money we can we can  bring bargains to travelers we can even  have a three percent reduction in price  at the time when the CPI went up 7  percent I think that's a terribly  important example next question please  yes sir I'm John Kendrick of George  Washington University and my question is  primarily for Rudy Oswald in view of the  preponderance of labor costs in price do  you think it's possible to wind down  inflation without reducing the rate of  increase in wage rates or average hourly  compensation and if you agree with that  let me ask you how would you recommend  that we gradually decelerate the rate of  increase in wage rates in the economy  dr. Kendrick I think that you've written  many books on the impact of productivity  in the economy and the ability to have  real gains in the economy as a result of  tivity well part of the gains of  productivity can be used to slow down  productivity when I spoke before in  terms of dr. Khan's notion of looking at  sectoral problems I think that there are  certain sectoral problems that can be  addressed that are not directly wage  related and in general wage changes in  the last year the last three years have  not been particularly pressing in terms  of pushing prices up there have been  substantial attempts to catch up but  that's an attempt to say that when  somebody works he expects to be able to  have the ability to purchase something  with what he works for rather than just  paper I just say one word about it I  have no intention of putting ascribing  blame particularly to one part of the  economy or another but you really have  missed the basic arithmetic point that  dr. Kendrick made that if 70 percent of  your sales prices or of your gross  national product goes to labor then  there's absolutely no way in the world  in which you can have wage increases of  10 11 9 or even 7 percent a year when we  have never come remotely close on any  long-range basis to producing  productivity increases of more than  three and now we're more nearly at the  2% level without getting something like  giving those different examples six  seven eight percent of inflation per  year and that's the simple answer to his  question there is no way and I guess  that's that's a matter of simple  arithmetic the other point I wanted to  make was catch up you will always find  at least 50 percent of the population  who have not done as well in a preceding  year is the other 50 percent actually if  you know the way these frequency  distributions are spread it's more than  50 percent to do less well than average  if you always always say anybody who is  falling behind has a right to catch up  then you have built in a permanent  engine of inflation catch up equals  inflation target question I certainly  agree with the point on arithmetic that  is there is no way in which you can get  inflation to slow down without having  menstruates slowdown in the rate of wage  increase but as I understand this  program something more is intended and  that is if I understood Fred kind  correctly or earlier what you saying is  that the program plans to look at  situations where competition in the  labor market is less prevalent now it  seems to me that that makes sense if you  can induce the institutional change that  makes the situation more competitive but  to use wage controls or wage standard or  some kind of wage price policy to get a  result that you would not get with the  conditions of competition in that  industry in that sector I think is  likely to fail but I think this is so  important that I forgive me for another  second please do we're asking where are  the signs of excessive aggregate demand  too much spending in the economy that is  pushing wages up and we see very little  signs of that in most parts of the  economy you may say it's there's  excessive spending for housing and yet  construction wages have risen much less  than the average and my answer is that  the basic problem of inflation is a  social problem it is a mark of a society  in which people are constantly  increasing their income claims both in  the wages they demand in the profit  margins on which that they add to their  wage cost in the demands that they place  on government for tax preferences or  subsidies in the demands for government  spending programs they all add up to  more than we can supply at constant  prices and that's what we've been  witnessing it's justified by some on the  grounds of catch-up it's justified by  others on the ground that there in  industries which are above average and  productivity  what we see then is a problem that it  seems to us is not easily solvable  simply by putting Mack aggregate  restraints because that will cause great  unemployment it's simple enough to throw  twenty million people out of work and  then they will moderate their wage  demands we have to do something by way  of inducing a general spirit of social  discipline which alone can come in my  judgment only by a recognition that  we're we're all like hamsters in a cage  running as fast as we can and staying in  the same place and that we I think  there's a general recognition that we've  all got a moderate in some way and I  don't know how to do that by simply  imposing monetary and fiscal restraints  without serious unemployment  let me just answer that very briefly  Kostas it seems to me that our problem  so far this year has not been a matter  of wages rising faster than prices or in  any sense pushing prices up it seems to  me clearly to not have come from the  side workers all saying to themselves we  can't fall behind that we got to catch  up or we got to do better instead it  seems to have come on the price side  that is to say prices have risen a good  deal more during the course of the year  then have wages it seems to me very  difficult to argue that that our problem  now is those costs pushing themselves up  autonomously rather than that it's  demand pushing prices up dr. Greenberg  the problem that we have got is one  which we have no firm theoretical  understanding of we have for the last  six or seven years been endeavoring to  understand the theoretical framework  which constructs a situation in which we  have chronic inflation in a period of  less than full utilization of resources  it is not an explanation to say that  there are excess demands in the social  system because that translated into the  mechanisms of an economy is a demand  pull situation the problem that we have  got is not something we fully understand  when we endeavor to create the  relationship of wages and prices in our  economic models we have found them  wholly inadequate we have been groping  for answers and I think at this stage  the results to date have been most  unsatisfactory I would not conclude from  that that therefore we then presume that  because we see a relationship between  wages and prices which does exist that  we can somehow treat the symptoms of the  problem and presume the issue will be  resolved I think the experience that we  have had in endeavoring to bring down  inflation by bringing down wages or  bringing down wages by bringing  prices invariably has failed and fallen  apart all that we can observe is that  these particular efforts at creating the  symptoms have not worked and I think  that to call for a particular form of  restraint which somehow simultaneously  will bring the whole system down  I think flies in the face of the  experiences that we and other countries  have had in this whole particular area  there has been one of the explanations  which is this issue of inflation and  expectations as though that explains the  phenomenon well it doesn't because the  mere fact of people expecting prices to  rise further will certainly induce them  to take certain actions but it's not  clear in which direction in fact our  experience when people are concerned  about inflation is that they don't spend  more they save more they can track their  actions which is actually an anti  inflationary force so that doesn't  explain it I think what we have is a  tremendously serious problem I am very  much concerned that we are we all grant  that there's some fundamental solutions  to this and we keep saying that we can't  we have to create 20 million unemployed  but that I find rather rhetorical to say  the least what concerns me is that we  know what the the problem is we say it  is politically socially and otherwise  unacceptable to do this that or the  other thing and we find that as we  procrastinate the problem gets worse and  the form of the socially and politically  unacceptable alternative gets worse  every year  I submit at some point we're going to  have to come to grips with this problem  and work very hard to resolve it and not  continuously look for solutions for  which there are no costs may I have the  next question  sir yes dance card feat Fairchild  publications when the president's  program was first announced he indicated  that the federal government would use  its purchasing power to encourage  compliance by not buying from  contractors who violated the guidelines  my question is are you moving toward  asking the nation's consumers to boycott  companies that violate the price  guidelines we haven't done so explicitly  and I'm sorry to say that I have not yet  reached the point of being able to give  you an adequate answer you must  recognize however that we what we have  is a program that is in very large  measure voluntary and is indeed being  enforced by something on the order of a  hundred people and we are determined to  fashion a program that which will not  require any more than 100 people there's  a tremendous amount of self enforcement  here now in those circumstances where  you're depending very largely on  volition and you're not going to have a  field of inspectors who are going to be  able to look at prices and individual  grocery stores or the rents charged or  the prices charged for haircuts I think  that that one very important enforcement  mechanism will have to be a kind of  sensitization of consumers to this  inflation and decisions by consumers not  to buy from people who are raising their  prices excessively well but I think  that's expecting something that the  guidelines don't even allow somebody to  know for example in November 16th  Newsweek magazine raised the price of  the magazine from a dollar to a dollar  and a quarter but there's no way of an  individual knowing whether that's in  compliance with the guidelines or not  and yet that's the 25 percent increase  in terms of a product that is sold on  the newsstands I think the expectation  that has been given by the president's  message is that there is a government  who is concern has set guidelines and  will do something and yet you tell them  on the wage side employers will do  something but on the price side nothing  will be done well I think that's absurd  I mean we don't have any authority any  more authority to do something about  wages in a particular locality then we  have to do about prices in that locality  so if you're going to talk about the  limitations of our enforcement  mechanisms they apply those limitations  apply both the wage in the price side  except that on the wage side you do have  an organized mechanism of enforcers  employers there is no organized group of  consumers oh that's not true please do  it right I think that the presumption  that employers somehow would like to  keep the wages down no matter what I  think  missus how wages are set in a non-union  area wages are set not by the  benevolence of the employer but by their  need to keep their good workers in place  producing the goods that they have to  sell to their customers if they  arbitrarily endeavour to restrain wages  below some particular market level they  would find a substantial part of their  most productive workforce going to their  competitors and I would think that that  view that somehow that the employers are  controlling the wages and are in fact  the wage enforces I think badly miss  reads how the system functions doctor  Costas I'd like to point out an oddity  about the way this kind of enforcement  mechanism works you have a situation  where the government is getting  procurement bids and so on and as I  understand it instead of taking the low  bid if that firm was out of compliance  because it granted a large wage  increases a large wage increase it would  take the higher bid it seems to me that  what we don't want to encourage is  behavior that has high prices and  doesn't raise wages very much and it  seems to me to be a mistake to encourage  consumers to to patronize that kind of  firm as well  alright this concludes another public  policy forum presented by the American  Enterprise Institute for Public Policy  Research this public policy forum on  weapons against inflation has brought  you the views of four experts in the  field it was presented by AEI the  American Enterprise Institute it is the  aim of AEI to clarify issues of the day  by presenting many viewpoints in the  hope that by so doing those who wish to  learn about the decision-making process  will benefit from such a free exchange  of informed and enlightened opinion this  public policy forum series is created  and supplied to this station as a public  service by the American Enterprise  Institute Washington DC for a transcript  of this program send $3.75 to the  American Enterprise Institute 11:50 17th  Street Northwest Washington DC to 0 0 3  6

